  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 8.1. Increased capacity of member States to develop new or reformed sustainable national social protection strategies, policies or legal frameworks to extend coverage and enhance benefit adequacy</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">On 28 March 2020, the Government of the Republic of Namibia gazetted Labour Directives relating to Covid-19: State of Emergency - Covid-19 Regulations relating to Sickness and employee compensation benefits.
The regulations require coverage of all employees who catch COVID-19 or suffer from a COVID-19 related post-traumatic stress disorder during the course of employment, enabling them to receive benefits from the Employees&apos; Compensation Fund in the context of the Employees Compensation Act, 1941 (Act No. 30 of 1941) for an industrial disease.
 The ILO provided technical advice and global good practices on responding to COVID-19 in the workplace through initiatives implemented jointly with the African Union Development Agency (AUDA-NEPAD), the Southern Africa Development Community and the African Regional Labour Administration Centre (ARLAC) from April 2020 to August 2021. The initiatives included webinars and training courses on promoting and managing OSH to help stop the spread of the COVID-19 virus in workplaces in which Namibian tripartite partners attended. In August 2020, Namibian officials were part of the experts that developed the AUDA-NEPAD Guidelines on COVID19 and OSH for domestication by member states.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">8.1.1. Number of member States with new or revised national social protection policies to extend coverage, enhance comprehensiveness and/or increase adequacy of benefits</narrative>
    </title>
